WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.376 --> 00:00:06.503
When I look at a flower,

00:00:06.545 --> 00:00:09.881
I don't see just a beautiful object
to put in a vase.

00:00:10.799 --> 00:00:13.885
I see an intelligent organism
that is not still.

00:00:14.636 --> 00:00:18.890
Perhaps it just opened its petals
to discover light for the first time.

00:00:18.890 --> 00:00:21.434
Or maybe it's at the end of its period,

00:00:21.476 --> 00:00:24.813
desperately blossoming to attract a bee
and get pollinated.

00:00:25.689 --> 00:00:29.609
As humans, we innately respond
to the blooming of a flower.

00:00:30.402 --> 00:00:33.321
But how do we design this exact feeling?

00:00:33.697 --> 00:00:36.950
How can we capture this visceral
response in people?

00:00:38.368 --> 00:00:43.665
I cofounded an artistic practice
to tap the mysteries of this world,

00:00:43.707 --> 00:00:46.876
not by studying, but by making.

00:00:46.876 --> 00:00:48.878
We called our studio "Drift"

00:00:48.920 --> 00:00:53.675
and wondered why almost everything
that is made by people is static.

00:00:53.717 --> 00:00:55.635
Because nature is never static.

00:00:56.344 --> 00:00:58.930
Our mission is informed by nature,

00:00:58.972 --> 00:01:01.600
and evolution is our process.

00:01:01.641 --> 00:01:03.977
That means we try and fail,

00:01:04.019 --> 00:01:06.938
make thousands of iterations
before we succeed.

00:01:08.732 --> 00:01:10.275
In 2006,

00:01:10.275 --> 00:01:12.777
when the studio was still the two of us,

00:01:12.777 --> 00:01:14.112
I had an idea.

00:01:15.697 --> 00:01:17.324
How cool would it be

00:01:17.365 --> 00:01:21.953
if suddenly little lights
would flow down from the ceiling,

00:01:21.995 --> 00:01:26.583
opening and closing,
interacting with us like flowers?

00:01:27.959 --> 00:01:31.463
I was longing for this feeling
to feel present and in the moment,

00:01:31.504 --> 00:01:34.215
the way how I feel
when I walk in a forest.

00:01:34.883 --> 00:01:36.509
The idea was maybe impractical

00:01:36.551 --> 00:01:41.222
because to make movement, we probably
had to work with motors and software

00:01:41.264 --> 00:01:44.434
and other tools and knowledge
that we didn't have.

00:01:44.893 --> 00:01:47.062
So we decided to start this project.

00:01:47.812 --> 00:01:50.607
In two weeks of kit bashing
vacuum cleaner parts

00:01:50.649 --> 00:01:52.901
and folding paper patterns,

00:01:52.901 --> 00:01:56.529
we have barely something moving,
but it was our deadline,

00:01:56.529 --> 00:01:59.324
so we brought our drafts
to a local exhibition.

00:02:00.659 --> 00:02:02.452
To our surprise,

00:02:02.452 --> 00:02:06.206
the shy opening and proudly
descending creatures

00:02:06.206 --> 00:02:07.999
immediately drew attention.

00:02:08.708 --> 00:02:13.755
People responded to them with wonder
and a smile on their face,

00:02:13.797 --> 00:02:15.882
almost as if there was
a puppy in the room.

00:02:16.883 --> 00:02:18.468
And we thought it was quite cool

00:02:18.510 --> 00:02:21.930
that with our art we could
change people’s behavior.

00:02:21.971 --> 00:02:24.432
So we decided to explore this further.

00:02:25.350 --> 00:02:28.561
Project "Shylight"
became a process of years.

00:02:29.396 --> 00:02:32.399
Coming from art school
with no technical background,

00:02:32.440 --> 00:02:37.028
we had to learn to build control boards,
develop programming software,

00:02:37.070 --> 00:02:42.117
make mechanics move in an organic way
with gravity and complex silk patterns.

00:02:42.158 --> 00:02:46.162
Basically, we had to become
engineers, programmers,

00:02:46.162 --> 00:02:50.125
seamstresses and choreographers
at the same time.

00:02:51.084 --> 00:02:54.087
But how do you express a motion

00:02:54.129 --> 00:02:57.132
between an acceleration factor
and a coordinate?

00:02:58.299 --> 00:03:00.760
Here is where we learned
the most important thing.

00:03:01.386 --> 00:03:03.888
(Music)

00:03:12.439 --> 00:03:15.442
It's not about that it moves.

00:03:16.234 --> 00:03:18.111
It's about how it moves.

00:03:18.862 --> 00:03:23.366
Our body responds automatically
to certain types of movement,

00:03:23.366 --> 00:03:26.035
movement we already know from nature.

00:03:26.745 --> 00:03:29.581
Rhythms that are programmed
deep within us.

00:03:35.211 --> 00:03:37.255
We are designed to be in a constant

00:03:37.297 --> 00:03:39.799
and dynamic interaction
with our environment.

00:03:40.717 --> 00:03:45.138
There are numerous phenomena in nature
that are super inspiring.

00:03:45.638 --> 00:03:47.265
But perhaps the most intriguing

00:03:47.265 --> 00:03:50.310
and relevant animal behavior
to me is murmuration.

00:03:51.186 --> 00:03:56.316
Imagine thousands of individuals
going places together,

00:03:56.357 --> 00:03:58.151
with no clear leader,

00:03:58.151 --> 00:03:59.861
without fighting,

00:04:00.612 --> 00:04:04.073
still following their impulses
and avoiding each other.

00:04:04.449 --> 00:04:06.701
Isn't this amazing?

00:04:07.076 --> 00:04:09.496
Because we are not capable of doing this.

00:04:10.622 --> 00:04:13.792
So dreaming big, we had this other idea.

00:04:14.709 --> 00:04:19.297
How cool would it be
if we could create a murmuration

00:04:19.339 --> 00:04:23.092
with hundreds of lights flying in the sky,

00:04:23.092 --> 00:04:25.512
responding to each other as birds?

00:04:26.846 --> 00:04:30.934
Well, you would think you could
hang a light under a drone.

00:04:30.975 --> 00:04:35.772
But back in 2008, drones didn't really
exist yet in the way they are today.

00:04:35.814 --> 00:04:39.818
Universities were testing,
in an early stage, quadcopters.

00:04:39.859 --> 00:04:43.029
And in our search
for collaborations and technologies

00:04:43.029 --> 00:04:47.659
to help get all these objects
in the sky at the same time,

00:04:47.700 --> 00:04:49.911
we didn't find anything.

00:04:50.620 --> 00:04:52.455
We were just way too early.

00:04:52.497 --> 00:04:55.667
But we were so excited about this idea.

00:04:55.667 --> 00:04:57.752
We just had to do this project.

00:04:58.253 --> 00:05:00.171
Not knowing that we would plant a seed

00:05:00.171 --> 00:05:03.591
for what almost ten years later
is becoming an industry.

00:05:06.928 --> 00:05:08.680
In our software development,

00:05:08.721 --> 00:05:11.432
I think this was quite a unique process.

00:05:11.432 --> 00:05:15.144
Different engineers basing
their code on scientific data,

00:05:15.186 --> 00:05:17.856
we developed our software as artists

00:05:17.897 --> 00:05:21.234
by observing swarms for hours and hours.

00:05:21.776 --> 00:05:26.197
Not calculating but relating
to the behavior of these birds.

00:05:26.656 --> 00:05:32.996
It was incredible how many uncontrollable
factors were influencing their choices.

00:05:33.955 --> 00:05:37.917
But it also made me realize,
and it was eye-opening actually,

00:05:37.917 --> 00:05:40.003
that also this happens to me.

00:05:40.044 --> 00:05:42.297
I don't have control over my life.

00:05:43.631 --> 00:05:44.966
With a computer, of course,

00:05:44.966 --> 00:05:49.053
you can test multiple scenarios
without big consequences.

00:05:49.095 --> 00:05:51.973
But of course, this was not
the physical reality.

00:05:53.182 --> 00:05:55.602
Well, imagine how excited we were

00:05:55.643 --> 00:05:59.355
when almost ten years later
this was no longer software.

00:05:59.397 --> 00:06:01.608
(Video) Ralph Nauta:
OK, this is a test one.

00:06:03.651 --> 00:06:06.195
Lonneke Gordijn: There they were, drones,

00:06:06.195 --> 00:06:08.364
one by one, appearing in the sky.

00:06:09.574 --> 00:06:11.701
Slowly starting to swarm.

00:06:14.704 --> 00:06:16.456
We were observing it.

00:06:17.916 --> 00:06:19.417
We were feeling it.

00:06:20.960 --> 00:06:22.921
And we felt nothing.

00:06:24.964 --> 00:06:26.090
Nothing.

00:06:26.132 --> 00:06:27.133
(Laughter)

00:06:27.175 --> 00:06:28.927
No emotional response.

00:06:28.968 --> 00:06:31.012
We remained blanco.

00:06:31.012 --> 00:06:32.180
What?

00:06:32.680 --> 00:06:34.599
On a computer, it looked great.

00:06:35.475 --> 00:06:38.061
But this swarm looked way too technical.

00:06:39.562 --> 00:06:42.899
And we had to go back
and let nature be our teacher

00:06:42.941 --> 00:06:46.527
because we were fighting
the nature of our technology.

00:06:47.487 --> 00:06:51.908
Well, in a creative process, you try,
you fail, you start over again.

00:06:52.575 --> 00:06:54.243
You change direction.

00:06:54.285 --> 00:06:57.038
Like evolution and like murmuration.

00:06:57.747 --> 00:06:59.248
And you're not in control,

00:06:59.290 --> 00:07:01.000
but you have to pave the road

00:07:01.000 --> 00:07:04.837
in this constant and dynamic relationship
with whatever that happens.

00:07:05.505 --> 00:07:08.091
You know when it's right,
when you feel it.

00:07:08.716 --> 00:07:11.177
You feel when the connection is made.

00:07:12.679 --> 00:07:16.641
So in 2018, we finally took
our artwork to Burning Man.

00:07:17.934 --> 00:07:21.312
(Music)

00:07:22.230 --> 00:07:23.690
The drones took off.

00:07:25.316 --> 00:07:26.776
And it was magic.

00:07:28.403 --> 00:07:33.116
The swarm, larger than a building,
was murmuring over the desert.

00:07:37.370 --> 00:07:40.540
It wasn't a sculpture or a performance.

00:07:41.207 --> 00:07:43.084
It was something bigger.

00:07:43.126 --> 00:07:46.379
It became an environment
that impacted the audience

00:07:46.421 --> 00:07:48.423
all at once in the same way.

00:07:49.424 --> 00:07:51.384
And together with the music,

00:07:51.384 --> 00:07:54.387
everyone melted into one energy.

00:07:55.805 --> 00:07:57.557
People were crying.

00:07:57.557 --> 00:07:58.808
I was crying.

00:07:59.726 --> 00:08:01.436
It was powerful.

00:08:02.520 --> 00:08:06.190
And this experience connected
all the present elements

00:08:06.190 --> 00:08:07.859
in that one moment.

00:08:07.900 --> 00:08:12.947
And it felt like a collective memory
that came from deep within our DNA.

00:08:13.448 --> 00:08:17.243
(Music)

00:08:27.211 --> 00:08:29.380
In nature, every environment --

00:08:29.422 --> 00:08:30.631
(Applause)

00:08:30.631 --> 00:08:31.841
Thank you.

00:08:31.841 --> 00:08:35.511
(Applause)

00:08:35.928 --> 00:08:39.140
In nature, every environment
is constantly moving.

00:08:39.599 --> 00:08:43.644
And an animal doesn't know
what it will eat tonight or at what time,

00:08:43.644 --> 00:08:45.605
but it will be present in the moment,

00:08:45.646 --> 00:08:48.316
ready to adapt to whatever is happening.

00:08:49.233 --> 00:08:52.862
So why are human-built
environments static?

00:08:53.404 --> 00:08:54.822
Nothing moves.

00:08:55.531 --> 00:08:59.535
Do we realize that this also
stops us from moving?

00:09:00.578 --> 00:09:03.331
Did we unlearn to deal with change?

00:09:04.832 --> 00:09:08.211
Is this why we stopped noticing
our environment?

00:09:09.754 --> 00:09:14.634
And is this maybe why we don't
respond to climate change,

00:09:14.634 --> 00:09:17.762
and we feel numb while this
is actually happening?

00:09:19.347 --> 00:09:22.391
Behind me, you'll see a block of concrete.

00:09:23.601 --> 00:09:25.728
I don't know if you noticed it before.

00:09:26.395 --> 00:09:29.565
But how do you feel
about a block of concrete?

00:09:31.025 --> 00:09:33.319
Is it necessary to feel anything at all?

00:09:33.361 --> 00:09:35.530
Because it's just a block of concrete.

00:09:36.489 --> 00:09:40.701
We are so used to be part
of a static world

00:09:40.743 --> 00:09:43.121
with concrete as its main character.

00:09:44.455 --> 00:09:46.415
But this shouldn't be our world.

00:09:47.333 --> 00:09:49.168
This is how we used to think,

00:09:49.210 --> 00:09:51.504
but this cannot be our future.

00:09:53.214 --> 00:09:57.218
What can we learn
if the world is not in our control?

00:09:58.970 --> 00:10:02.807
What can we learn
if we disrupt our expectations?

00:10:07.645 --> 00:10:11.816
Well, at the moment the reality
is just changing here in front of you.

00:10:14.986 --> 00:10:16.737
Can you accept what you see?

00:10:18.447 --> 00:10:20.116
And how does this feel?

00:10:22.368 --> 00:10:23.578
Is it frightening?

00:10:26.914 --> 00:10:32.253
Or can you feel a sense of wonder
for a block of concrete?

00:10:37.049 --> 00:10:39.510
Can you imagine there would be a moment

00:10:39.510 --> 00:10:42.221
that you feel open

00:10:42.221 --> 00:10:45.308
to have a connection
with a block of concrete?

00:10:56.444 --> 00:11:02.200
(Cheers and applause)

00:11:05.328 --> 00:11:07.997
How can we deal with a changing world?

00:11:08.789 --> 00:11:10.208
Are we in control?

00:11:12.793 --> 00:11:16.172
I never felt at home in the static world.

00:11:16.172 --> 00:11:17.840
And since my childhood,

00:11:17.882 --> 00:11:21.969
I felt that animal behavior
and plant behavior

00:11:22.011 --> 00:11:25.598
is way more logical
than the way people behave.

00:11:25.640 --> 00:11:31.270
(Laughter)

00:11:38.611 --> 00:11:42.531
As artists, we develop artworks

00:11:42.573 --> 00:11:46.410
that use movement to open us up

00:11:46.410 --> 00:11:49.747
and make us feel safe to embrace change.

00:11:50.289 --> 00:11:52.375
Because there is one fact.

00:11:53.709 --> 00:11:55.211
Change is coming.

00:11:55.962 --> 00:11:58.297
And we are not in control.

00:11:59.131 --> 00:12:00.883
We are murmuring.

00:12:01.592 --> 00:12:03.135
We are drifting.

00:12:04.637 --> 00:12:05.846
Thank you.

00:12:05.846 --> 00:12:12.812
(Cheers and applause)

00:12:21.445 --> 00:12:24.323
Helen Walters: So don't worry,
it'll be here all session.

00:12:24.323 --> 00:12:25.491
But just ...

00:12:25.491 --> 00:12:26.492
(Laughter)

00:12:26.534 --> 00:12:29.328
Lonneke, that was amazing.

00:12:29.370 --> 00:12:31.330
I'm really sorry, you guys.

00:12:31.914 --> 00:12:34.542
It's on its way out, it's fine.

00:12:34.542 --> 00:12:35.835
(Laughter)

00:12:35.876 --> 00:12:37.420
Okay, so come over here.

00:12:37.920 --> 00:12:41.090
Just as long as we're fine, that's fine.

00:12:41.090 --> 00:12:42.174
(Laughter)

00:12:42.216 --> 00:12:43.426
LG: We're safe.

00:12:46.012 --> 00:12:47.471
HW: What's happening?

00:12:48.055 --> 00:12:49.181
LG: Well ...

00:12:49.557 --> 00:12:52.059
This drifter tries to find its way out.

00:12:52.101 --> 00:12:53.853
HW: It's trying to find its way out.

00:12:53.853 --> 00:12:55.146
Is it sentient?

00:12:55.813 --> 00:12:57.898
LG: Well, it should know where it goes.

00:12:57.940 --> 00:12:59.108
So let's see.

00:12:59.108 --> 00:13:00.901
HW: No, but really, what's happening?

00:13:00.943 --> 00:13:02.111
What is going on?

00:13:02.111 --> 00:13:03.529
(Laughter)

00:13:03.529 --> 00:13:04.780
LG: I mean,

00:13:04.822 --> 00:13:07.116
this is kind of magic, I would say,

00:13:07.116 --> 00:13:10.286
and a big piece of engineering
for my colleagues

00:13:10.286 --> 00:13:13.456
and amazing engineers
that are making this happen.

00:13:13.456 --> 00:13:15.458
(Applause)

00:13:15.499 --> 00:13:16.792
Yeah.

00:13:16.792 --> 00:13:19.295
HW: OK, so, with this,

00:13:19.295 --> 00:13:21.380
you basically just came to the TED stage

00:13:21.380 --> 00:13:26.635
and told a whole bunch
of type-A people to cede control,

00:13:26.677 --> 00:13:28.012
to like, give up control.

00:13:28.012 --> 00:13:31.807
Like, do you have any advice
for how we should do that,

00:13:31.849 --> 00:13:35.144
especially when there's something concrete
floating over our heads?

00:13:35.186 --> 00:13:38.564
No, but like, seriously, like,
your work is about trial and error.

00:13:38.564 --> 00:13:40.149
It's about experimentation.

00:13:40.191 --> 00:13:42.109
Do you have any tips for the rest of us?

00:13:42.151 --> 00:13:43.819
I mean, not me, but these guys.

00:13:43.861 --> 00:13:45.654
LG: (Laughs)

00:13:45.654 --> 00:13:49.867
Well, I think it's an illusion
that we have control.

00:13:50.201 --> 00:13:53.204
And if you look at nature,
you see that it's also not there.

00:13:53.245 --> 00:13:58.209
And I think we have to accept who we are,

00:13:58.209 --> 00:14:00.753
that we are not in control
and that we need to change.

00:14:00.795 --> 00:14:03.005
You know, our bodies are made to change,

00:14:03.047 --> 00:14:04.548
but we kind of forgot about it.

00:14:04.548 --> 00:14:09.762
We built our safety structure around us,
and we need to learn to go with it

00:14:09.762 --> 00:14:12.014
and change with it as we are changing

00:14:12.056 --> 00:14:13.349
and this world is changing.

00:14:13.349 --> 00:14:14.892
HW: Lonneke, thank you so much.

00:14:14.934 --> 00:14:16.185
LG: Thank you very much.

00:14:16.185 --> 00:14:17.728
(Applause)

